1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,100
Tutor: Ah... come in, Russ.

2
00:00:04,100 --> 00:00:05,900
Russ: Thank you.

3
00:00:05,900 --> 00:00:10,648
Tutor: Now you wanted to consult me about your class presentation on nanotechnology -

4
00:00:10,648 --> 00:00:14,300
you're due to give it in next week, aren't you?

5
00:00:14,300 --> 00:00:18,023
Russ: That's right. And I'm really struggling. I chose the topic

6
00:00:18,023 --> 00:00:22,100
because I didn't know much about it and wanted to learn more

7
00:00:22,100 --> 00:00:24,792
but now I've read so much about it,

8
00:00:24,792 --> 00:00:26,620
in a way there's too much to say

9
00:00:26,620 --> 00:00:31,900
I could talk for much longer than the twenty minutes I've been allocated.

10
00:00:31,900 --> 00:00:35,020
Should I assume the other students don't know much

11
00:00:35,020 --> 00:00:38,426
and give them a kind of general introduction,

12
00:00:38,426 --> 00:00:43,800
or should I try and make them share my fascination with a particular aspect?

13
00:00:43,800 --> 00:00:48,900
Tutor: You could do either, but you'll need to have it clear in your own mind.

14
00:00:48,900 --> 00:00:52,600
Russ: Then I think I'll give an overview.

15
00:00:52,600 --> 00:00:59,700
Tutor: OK. Now, one way of approaching this is to work through developments in chronological order.

16
00:00:59,700 --> 00:01:01,100
Russ: Uh-huh.

17
00:01:01,100 --> 00:01:07,200
Tutor: On the other hand, you could talk about the numerous ways that nanotechnology is being applied.

18
00:01:07,200 --> 00:01:13,470
Russ: You mean things like thin films on camera displays to make them water-repellent,

19
00:01:13,470 --> 00:01:17,500
and additives to make motorcycle helmets stronger and lighter.

20
00:01:17,500 --> 00:01:25,576
Tutor: Exactly. Or another way would be to focus on its impact in one particular area,

21
00:01:25,576 --> 00:01:28,100
say medicine, or space exploration.

22
00:01:28,100 --> 00:01:33,100
Russ: That would make it easier to focus. Perhaps I should do that.

23
00:01:33,100 --> 00:01:36,000
Tutor: I think that would be a good idea.

24
00:01:36,000 --> 00:01:42,100
Russ: Right. How important is it to include slides in the presentation?

25
00:01:42,100 --> 00:01:44,717
Tutor: They aren't essential, by any means.

26
00:01:44,717 --> 00:01:50,700
And there's a danger of tailoring what you say to fit whatever slides you can find.

27
00:01:50,700 --> 00:01:53,360
While it can be good to include slides

28
00:01:53,360 --> 00:01:57,350
you could end up spending too long looking for suitable ones.

29
00:01:57,350 --> 00:02:00,500
You might find it better to leave them out.

30
00:02:00,500 --> 00:02:05,730
Russ: I see. Another thing I was wondering about was how to start.

31
00:02:05,730 --> 00:02:10,959
I know presentations often begin with 'First I'm going to talk about this,

32
00:02:10,959 --> 00:02:12,100
and then I'll talk about that

33
00:02:12,620 --> 00:02:18,200
but I thought about asking the audience what they know about nanotechnology.

34
00:02:18,200 --> 00:02:22,777
Tutor: That would be fine if you had an hour or two for the presentation,

35
00:02:22,777 --> 00:02:27,049
but you might find that you can't do anything with the answers you get,

36
00:02:27,049 --> 00:02:30,100
and it simply eats into the short time that's available.

37
00:02:30,100 --> 00:02:37,300
Russ: So, maybe I should mention a particular way that nanotechnology is used, to focus people's attention.

38
00:02:37,300 --> 00:02:39,600
Tutor: That sounds sensible.

39
00:02:39,600 --> 00:02:41,700
Russ: What do you think I should do next

40
00:02:42,140 --> 00:02:46,400
I really have to plan the presentation today and tomorrow.

41
00:02:46,400 --> 00:02:51,393
Tutor: Well, initially I think you should ignore all the notes you've made,

42
00:02:51,393 --> 00:02:52,480
take a small piece of paper

43
00:02:52,480 --> 00:02:58,307
and write a single short sentence that ties together the whole presentation:

44
00:02:58,307 --> 00:03:03,300
it can be something as simple as 'Nanotechnology is already improving our lives'.

45
00:03:03,300 --> 00:03:05,980
Then start planning the content around that

46
00:03:05,980 --> 00:03:10,400
You can always modify that sentence later, if you need to.

47
00:03:10,400 --> 00:03:12,000
Russ: OK.

48
00:03:12,000 --> 00:03:16,269
Tutor: OK, now let's think about actually giving the presentation.

49
00:03:16,269 --> 00:03:19,960
You've only given one before, if I remember correctly

50
00:03:19,960 --> 00:03:23,100
about an experiment you'd been involved in.

51
00:03:23,100 --> 00:03:26,800
Russ: That's right. It was pretty rubbish!

52
00:03:26,800 --> 00:03:31,158
Tutor: Let's say it was better in some respects than in others.

53
00:03:31,158 --> 00:03:32,140
With regard to the structure

54
00:03:32,140 --> 00:03:36,968
I felt that you ended rather abruptly, without rounding it off.

55
00:03:36,968 --> 00:03:40,600
Be careful not to do that in next week's presentation.

56
00:03:40,600 --> 00:03:42,300
Russ: OK.

57
00:03:42,300 --> 00:03:45,947
Tutor: And you made very little eye contact with the audience,

58
00:03:45,947 --> 00:03:49,926
because you were looking down at your notes most of the time.

59
00:03:49,926 --> 00:03:54,900
You need to be looking at the audience and only occasionally glancing at your notes.

60
00:03:54,900 --> 00:03:56,300
Russ: Mmm.

61
00:03:56,300 --> 00:03:58,631
Tutor: Your body language was a little odd.

62
00:03:58,631 --> 00:04:00,380
Every time you showed a slide,

63
00:04:00,380 --> 00:04:03,600
you turned your back on the audience so you could look at it

64
00:04:03,600 --> 00:04:06,500
you should have been looking at your laptop.

65
00:04:06,500 --> 00:04:08,500
And you kept scratching your head

66
00:04:08,500 --> 00:04:12,411
so I found myself wondering when you were next going to do that,

67
00:04:12,411 --> 00:04:14,900
instead of listening to what you were saying!

68
00:04:14,900 --> 00:04:18,448
Russ: Oh dear. What did you think of the language?

69
00:04:18,448 --> 00:04:22,100
I knew that not everyone was familiar with the subject

70
00:04:22,100 --> 00:04:25,900
so I tried to make it as simple as I could.

71
00:04:25,900 --> 00:04:31,900
Tutor: Yes, that came across. You used a few words that are specific to the field,

72
00:04:31,900 --> 00:04:33,580
but you always explained what they meant

73
00:04:33,580 --> 00:04:37,900
so the audience wouldn't have had any difficulty understanding.

74
00:04:37,900 --> 00:04:39,300
Russ: Uh-huh.

75
00:04:39,300 --> 00:04:43,311
Tutor: I must say the handouts you prepared were well thought out.

76
00:04:43,311 --> 00:04:45,700
They were a good summary of your presentation

77
00:04:45,700 --> 00:04:49,329
which people would be able to refer to later on.

78
00:04:49,329 --> 00:04:51,000
So well done on that.

79
00:04:51,000 --> 00:04:52,700
Russ: Thank you.

80
00:04:52,700 --> 00:04:56,700
Tutor: Well, I hope that helps you with next week's presentation.

81
00:04:56,700 --> 00:04:59,800
Russ: Yes, it will. Thanks a lot.

82
00:04:59,800 --> 00:05:04,000
Tutor: I'll look forward to seeing a big improvement, then.
